This is a typographic archive page — dark as a tin-type, amber as old postcards, set in EB Garamond and Fira Code. The hero types its title through a real character-by-character typewriter, then four scattered polaroids fade up around it like a postcard pile nudged open. Hover any polaroid and it straightens and lifts, the way you'd pull one card out to look at it closely.

The 25 limericks live in a two-column postcard grid below, each card with a tiny random rotation (±2°) that flattens on hover, the AABBA short lines (3rd and 4th) indented and italic so the rhyme scheme is visible at a glance. The keywords land cleanly: **economist** anchors Bill No. 02 (and reappears in the manifesto and a hero preview card); **Jurgen** arrives with Bill No. 23 and his blue donkeys; **bill** is, of course, everywhere — in every line of every card. Motion is restrained — no pulses, no gradients, no flashing. Just the typewriter cadence, the postcard stagger, fade-ups on scroll, and the hover that straightens a card. Reduced-motion users get the typed title instantly; touch users skip the parallax.

